      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're correct that the stability of beta-crystals (specifically Form V) is crucial for preventing bloom, and yes, errors often occur in the cooling phase if the temperature drops too quickly or fluctuates. When cocoa butter isn't maintained at an even, stable temperature during cooling, less stable crystal forms (Alpha or Beta I) develop and later migrate to the surface. Additionally, the type of cocoa butter matters significantly; natural butter is more prone to Bloom than American or EU-style chemically treated versions, so supplier differences can impact your results. To fix this, closely monitor your final cooling stage to ensure a steady descent to storage temperature without rewarming, and consider switching to Brazilian cocoa butter for a more stable crystallization.",
